From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ThumbFree <thumbfree@localhost>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 22:21:16 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] parakeet: decoder thread count of its own, pred projection
 kept across blank steps

Each RNN-T step runs the joint graph (6 nodes) and, after a token, the
predictor graph on the decoder's backend. With the session's threads (5 on
a Pixel 10) every node ends in a barrier across them while the matmuls are
small (2560x640 at most), so on the phone a step is mostly
synchronization: 83 ms for JFK's 177 steps, several times what their
memory traffic needs. The step graphs now take their thread count from
TRANSCRIBE_DECODER_THREADS=N (unset or 0: the session's count, as before,
until a phone sweep picks the default). The steps keep a persistent pool at
any count; with one thread it has no workers, so a step has no barrier and
ggml makes no pool per graph. The encoder projection before the loop, one
GEMM, runs on the session's count at every loop count. The joint's pred
projection (pred_w @ h + pred_b) moves
into the predictor graph, so a blank step, which keeps the predictor
state, reuses it instead of recomputing a 640x640 matmul. Tokens identical
on the 11 public clips for Parakeet Unified (RNN-T) and TDT v2 at 1, 2 and
5 loop threads. On a Mac, where barriers are cheap, one thread is slower
(JFK 5.2 to 11.4 ms).
